      "writtenOn": "2021-06-16T21:29:10Z",
      "side": 0,
      "message": "* The \"how\" is all implementation details.  For an example of the types of things that can change between API levels see http://cs/android/bionic/libc/arch-common/bionic/crtbegin.c?l\u003d68\u0026rcl\u003d552e97af2072089c7cc03bb82d6193a15961f8a0.  That is in crtbegin.c, but similar types of things may be done in clang - section alignments, new relocation types, etc.  Often these are new features that are more optimal in new platforms, but can\u0027t be used if your min_sdk_version is set below where they were added.\n\n* What you described is the old way, only symbols from your Sdk_version existed and you would have to use dlsym to access anything newer.  The new way involves clang automatically turning the symbols added between Min_sdk_version and Sdk_version into weak symbols, with static analysis to ensure proper runtime availability checks.  See go/android-future-symbols",
      "parentUuid": "a5ae03bc_ec55007b",
